      "problem": "Let $a=83$ and $n=6.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $r=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $83$ by $6$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n13<83÷6<14.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=13.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by computing $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=83-(13)(6)=5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $83=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}83={{13}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 6+{{5}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r=5.$",
